An archived colour space carries its model in the profile.

-[NSColorSpace initWithCoder:] did not exist, so every archived colour space raised
UNRECOGNIZED. Counted across captures: 209 of them, all in Swift Publisher.

WHAT THE ARCHIVE ACTUALLY CARRIES, counted over 568 NSColorSpace objects in the nibs of four
applications:

    NSID   NSModel   ICC data colour space   count
    9      0         GRAY                    536
    7      absent    RGB                      32

Every one of the 568 carries an NSICC profile, and bytes 16 to 20 of an ICC header are its data
colour space, so the profile decides the model and nothing has to be guessed.

NSMODEL IS ABSENT ON EVERY RGB ONE. Decoding it with the usual zero default would have made all 32
of them Gray, which is the trap of reading a missing key as a value. The default here is RGB and
the profile overrides it, which is right for both shapes in the archive.

This port has no ICC support, so a device colour space of the profile model is as much as can be
honoured. That is a smaller answer than macOS gives and it is a far larger one than raising.

NOT EXERCISED BY ANY CURRENT DRIVE, and I will not pretend otherwise. The 209 raises are all in
captures from 2026-09-05, three weeks of change ago. Today the settle drive reaches none of them,
and neither does the MoneyMoney About window, whose nib holds two of these objects that are never
instantiated on that path. So this is derived from the archive and from the failing selector, and
the drive that exercises it has not been found yet. CIDER_TRACE_COLORSPACE prints a CIDER_CSDECODE
line for every decode, which is how the next person will know when one happens.

